# Sets of programs: tau constants that read inputs, combined at the program level.
#
# See demos/README.md for how to run this script.
## Needs ltlsynt (from Spot) on the PATH.

# A specification denotes the set of causal programs that satisfy it on every
# input sequence. As an element of the Tau algebra, { S }:tau is that set, and
# the Boolean operations of the term are the operations on sets of programs:
# E' is the programs outside E, E1 & E2 the programs in both, E1 | E2 the
# programs in one of them, E = 0 says E has no program and E = 1 that every
# program is in E. The complement of a constant that reads an input is not the
# negation of its specification: a program is outside { always o1 = i1 } as
# soon as one input sequence makes it differ from the copier.

# The copier is not the constant-zero program, so a program is in the copier's
# set and outside the zero program's:

realizable { always o1[t]:sbf = i1[t]:sbf }:tau & { always o1[t]:sbf = 0 }:tau'

# The two sets differ, which the equality of the constants says too:

sat { always o1[t]:sbf = i1[t]:sbf }:tau = { always o1[t]:sbf = 0 }:tau

# Copying entails answering a 1 with a 1: no copier is outside that set.

realizable { always o1[t]:sbf = i1[t]:sbf }:tau & { always (i1[t]:sbf = 1 -> o1[t]:sbf = 1) }:tau'

# A union holds the programs that are in one side for every input. Neither the
# always-1 nor the always-0 program mirrors the input, so the union meets the
# mirror in no program, although the disjunction of the two specifications
# could pick a side after reading each input:

realizable ({ always o1[t]:sbf = 1 }:tau | { always o1[t]:sbf = 0 }:tau) & { always ((i1[t]:sbf = 1) <-> (o1[t]:sbf = 1)) }:tau

# E = 0 is unrealizability and E = 1 is validity, every input read as an
# output. No program keeps an input at 1 at some step whatever the input does,
# so that set is empty; the complement of "the input stays 0" is every program,
# since no program keeps an input at 0; and a set is everything only when
# every program satisfies the specification, which one that constrains an
# output does not:

sat { sometimes i1[t]:sbf = 1 }:tau = 0
sat { always i1[t]:sbf = 0 }:tau' = 1
sat { always (i1[t]:sbf = 0 || o1[t]:sbf = 1) }:tau = 1

# A constant with no temporal operator is read as its always, as a
# specification is:

realizable { o1[t]:sbf = i1[t]:sbf }:tau | { o1[t]:sbf = 0 }:tau

# A complement or a union of constants that read an input is compiled into
# one specification with hidden streams of the system's own: a complement
# gets one prediction per input stream, w_ps<n>_d_<input>, and the clause that
# the specification fails on the predicted inputs; a union gets a selector,
# w_ps<n>_b. ltl names them first, so its trace can be read:

ltl { always o1[t]:sbf = i1[t]:sbf }:tau & { always o1[t]:sbf = 0 }:tau'

# A run plays the compiled specification. The hidden streams are solved every
# step and never printed; i1 is read from the console here, one value per step:

run 4 steps { always o1[t]:sbf = i1[t]:sbf }:tau & { always o1[t]:sbf = 0 }:tau'.
0
1
1
0

# A set with no program cannot be run: a copier that is also outside the
# copier's set does not exist.

run 2 steps { always o1[t]:sbf = i1[t]:sbf }:tau & { always o1[t]:sbf = i1[t]:sbf }:tau'

# Three negations mean different things: ! inside a specification negates a
# step formula, ' on a tau constant is the complement of its set of programs,
# and - in the CTL* fragment (demo_6.5) is the negated realizability verdict.

quit
